<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Federal Republic of Mexico</h1><p class="st-meta">1846 CE – 1863 CE · capital Mexico City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The restored federal Mexico of 1846 to 1863, which lost half its territory to the United States and then fought the Reform War over church and state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>With war against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> beginning, a revolt in August 1846 overthrew the centralist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-paredes">Mariano Paredes</a> and restored the federal Constitution of 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> returned from exile to lead the defence, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>American armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in September 1847, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> of 1848 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">New Mexico</a> and the claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Santa Anna</a>'s final dictatorship from 1853 sold more land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a>. The liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-of-ayutla">Revolution of Ayutla</a> removed him in 1855 and launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-reforma">La Reforma</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-juarez">Ley Juárez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-lerdo">Ley Lerdo</a> curbed church and corporate privileges, and the Constitution of 1857 followed. Conservatives revolted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> won the Reform War of 1858 to 1861.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Juárez</a> suspended payments on foreign debts in 1861, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> intervened. Mexican forces beat the French at Puebla on 5 May 1862, but they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in June 1863, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a> was proclaimed, while Juárez kept a republican government on the move.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-federal-republic-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Second Federal Republic of Mexico" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Ludovicus Ferdinandus can have elements by Sodacan and Heralder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_la_Segunda_Rep%C3%BAblica_Federal_de_los_Estados_Unidos_Mexicanos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1846 CE – 1863 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mexico City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antonio López de Santa Anna, Ignacio Comonfort, Benito Juárez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Mexican States (1846-1863)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=second-federal-republic-of-mexico" aria-label="Second Federal Republic of Mexico on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2001 1461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Federal Republic of Mexico lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2321 1717L2321 1717M2321 1717L2570 1008M2321 1717L2321 1717M2321 1717L2321 1717M2321 1717L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2570 1008h